You are supporting a process mining research prototype.
Use only the synthetic scenario information provided below.
Do not invent company-specific system names, product names, confidential logs, internal metrics, or real organizational details.
Return valid JSON only. Do not wrap it in Markdown.

Scenario ID: S06
Scenario title: Vendor Meeting and Improvement Action Follow-up
Initial workflow description:
The team meets with an equipment vendor to discuss improvement ideas. After the meeting, someone summarizes the discussion and follows up on action items.

Domain expert answers:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "Who is responsible for writing the meeting summary after the vendor meeting, and is that role always the same person or does it rotate among attendees?",
      "answer": "The meeting organizer is responsible for recording decisions and open questions during the meeting and then sharing the meeting summary afterward. Whether this role rotates among attendees is not specified in the workflow."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "What is the expected format and destination of the meeting summary — for example, is it a structured template stored in a shared document system, sent as an email, or logged in a task-tracking tool?",
      "answer": "The meeting summary is shared by the meeting organizer and stored in a shared document repository. Meeting notes are also referenced as a system. The specific format or template used is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "Which tools or systems are currently used to assign, track, and update the status of action items that come out of the vendor meeting?",
      "answer": "An action-item tracker is the designated system for tracking action items. The manager assigns owners and due dates, and action-item owners update progress. The specific tool behind the action-item tracker is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "What criteria determine that an action item is complete, and at what point is an overdue item escalated and to whom?",
      "answer": "Completion criteria are not specified. A missed follow-up deadline is listed as a recognized exception, indicating overdue items are a known risk, but the escalation threshold and the person to whom escalation occurs are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "How often do vendor meetings occur — are they on a fixed recurring schedule, triggered by specific events such as equipment issues, or arranged on an ad hoc basis?",
      "answer": "The workflow is triggered by an improvement request or equipment issue summary, suggesting event-driven scheduling rather than a fixed recurring cadence. Whether meetings also occur on a regular schedule is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "Do any of the improvement actions agreed upon in the meeting require internal approval — such as from a manager or budget holder — before follow-up work can begin?",
      "answer": "The manager assigns action-item owners and due dates after decisions are recorded, which implies managerial involvement before follow-up work begins. Whether formal budget approval is also required is not specified."
    }
  ]
}
